	doi = {10.3390/en13102465},
	abstract = {A high-performance 20 {kW}, 20 Nm, 8000 rpm, spoke-type interior permanent magnet motor to be integrated into a {FormulaStudent} electric car’s powertrain has been designed to meet demanding performance requirements for its driving cycle. This paper describes key steps in the design optimization, analysis, fabrication, and testing of this machine. Design optimization used the non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm {II} ({NSGA}-{II}) coupled with a hybrid analytical/ﬁnite element model to reduce the computational time, achieving a torque and eﬃciency of 20 Nm and 98.6\%, respectively. A prototype has been constructed. The ﬁnal motor design has been tested, where experimental nominal torque and eﬃciency have reached 18.2 Nm and 90\%, respectively. Design challenges regarding the manufacturing are presented, justiﬁed, and discussed in detail. Test results were conducted to evaluate reliability and motor temperatures with and without water refrigeration at nominal current. Despite those adjustments in the optimized design, one shows that the impact on the car’s lap time was low, going from 77.3 s for the ideal optimized motor to 78.9 s for the prototyped motor.},
	pages = {2465},
